                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Crudelis is a short adventure RPG set in a strange steampunk version of 1896, and I think the main idea is easily the most interesting thing about it. I play as Henry Shackelton, a scientist who is working on a machine that could bring the dead back to life. Things go very wrong when his home is attacked and he is left with only 60 minutes to live. From there I have to investigate what happened, talk to people around the city and work out who I can trust before my time runs out. It is a small game, but the time limit gives the whole thing a nice sense of urgency.\n\nWhat I like most is that I am not simply following one straight story. There are 12 possible endings and my choices can change what happens later, so I have to pay attention to what people tell me and decide which problems are actually worth dealing with. The city of Abarian Creek has different locations to explore, and I can choose what I want to investigate during my limited time. I like this because it makes the short playtime feel more deliberate. I am constantly thinking about whether I should follow one lead or spend my remaining time looking into something else.\n\nThe setting is probably the strongest part of the game for me. The late 19th century steampunk world has a nice mixture of industrial technology, social unrest and darker historical themes. There are little details around the city that make it feel more interesting, including books, old technology, weapons and the workers strike at the factory. The handmade graphics are simple, but they fit the RPG Maker style and give the locations their own identity. The piano and cello soundtrack also does a good job of keeping the atmosphere serious without becoming too dramatic.\n\nThe problem is that the game can be frustrating when I am trying to work out what I am supposed to do. The journal helps, but it does not always explain things clearly enough, so I can end up wandering around the city checking every location and talking to people again. Some important objects also require me to look closely around the environment, which makes the investigation feel a little awkward at times. I do not mind having to think about a puzzle, but there are moments where I feel like I am searching for the right trigger rather than actually solving a mystery.\n\nThe short structure is also both a strength and a weakness. A single run is designed to take around an hour, but the game expects me to replay sections to discover its different outcomes. I actually like the idea because my decisions have more weight when I know I cannot see everything in one playthrough. The downside is that repeating the early parts can become tiring, especially when I already know what I need to do and still have to go through the same conversations and events again. The limited ways to recover from certain mistakes can make this even more annoying.\n\nI also think the game shows its age in some areas. The interface can be clunky, the English text has some awkward moments and there are occasional technical issues that can interrupt the experience. There is no traditional manual save system that lets me freely create a save before every decision, so I have to be more careful about how I approach each run. None of these problems completely destroy the game for me, but they make the investigation less smooth than the interesting story concept deserves. I think the game would have been much stronger if it had made experimenting with different choices easier.\n\nOverall, Crudelis is a very original little adventure that I think is held back more by its execution than its ideas. I really like the 60 minute concept, the steampunk setting, the branching story and the mystery surrounding Henry's final hour. The clunky interface, repeated sections and occasionally unclear objectives make it harder to enjoy than it should be, but there is enough personality here for me to appreciate what it is trying to do. I would not call it a perfect detective game, but for a free RPG Maker adventure with multiple endings, I think it is worth giving a chance if I want something short and different.\n\nRating: 7.6/10",
